Subject: [PATCH RFC v4 0/3] net: flow_dissector: byte-identical fast-paths for PPPoE, single-label MPLS, IP-in-IP

This series adds three more byte-identical fast-path shapes on top of
the v3 series (eth_ip, vlan, qinq; in net-next as commits 74c6ea03a417
.. 8504d0dca46b). Each shape is a separate static_branch with its own
sysctl under /proc/sys/net/flow_dissector/ and is disabled by default.
Each produces output byte-identical to the slow path for its shape.

This series excludes the descent shapes that change behaviour (the v3
vxlan_inner RFC EXPERIMENT, and the GRE, Geneve, and GTP-U inner
shapes), so that the byte-identical shapes can be reviewed separately.

Shapes:
  - PPPoE session + IPv4/IPv6 + TCP/UDP. PPPoE is a common L2 on ISP
    DSL and FTTH links. The slow path runs __skb_header_pointer and a
    per-field switch on each packet. Patch 1 reads the session header
    directly and calls the eth_ip fast-path for the PPP_IP and PPP_IPV6
    inner cases. Other PPP protocols (PPP_MPLS_UC, PPP_MPLS_MC, and
    PFC-compressed) return to the slow path.
  - Single-label MPLS + IP. Patch 2 handles the bottom-of-stack
    (BoS=1) single-label case in straight-line form. Multi-label stacks
    return to the slow path.
  - IP-in-IP (IPv4-in-IPv4, IPv6-in-IPv4, and IPv6-outer variants).
    Patch 3 descends into the inner IP when the outer protocol is
    IPPROTO_IPIP or IPPROTO_IPV6. The inner overwrites the outer address
    and basic keys (matching the slow path) and sets
    FLOW_DIS_ENCAPSULATION.

When a gate is disabled, its shape adds one not-taken branch on its
cold path. The eth_ip hot path is unchanged.

Safety:
  - Each shape either writes the same flow keys as the slow path, or it
    returns false and the slow path runs. Output was verified equal to
    the slow path for each shape.

Measurements. Dissector cost in isolation, userspace A/B test, in
nanoseconds per packet, measured across seven microarchitectures
(x86 Zen1, Zen2, Skylake; ARM Cortex-A53, A72, A76; RISC-V X60). Range
of the reduction across those microarchitectures:

    PPPoE     -8.6% to -28.0%   read session header, call eth_ip
    MPLS      -6.7% to -15.0%   single-label read; with KEY_MPLS not
                                requested both paths then stop, so this
                                measures the label read only
    IP-in-IP  -36.8% to -47.0%  largest reduction; the slow path re-runs
                                the full parser for the inner IP, the
                                fast-path does a second straight-line
                                IP read

Live-traffic measurements are within run-to-run noise (link- or
queue-bound), consistent with v3. Full per-shape and per-architecture
matrix and raw logs:
https://github.com/randomizedcoder/xdp2 , under perf-results/2026-06-25-*

Base: stacks on the byte-identical subset of v3 (commit 8504d0dca46b).
Independent of the vxlan_inner RFC EXPERIMENT.

Open questions:
  - Multi-label MPLS: a separate sysctl (mpls_deep, disabled by
    default), or fold it into the mpls gate with an auto-toggle like
    qinq/vlan? We prefer a separate gate. The qinq/vlan auto-toggle
    reflects a strict dependency (qinq requires vlan) that does not
    apply here.
  - IP-in-IP handles only the standalone IPIP and IPv6 protocols
    (tunnels from "ip link add type ipip" / "type ip6tnl"). It does not
    handle GRE-encapsulated IP-in-IP (a separate shape). Is the
    standalone gate too narrow without GRE for any deployment?
  - PPPoE writes FLOW_DISSECTOR_KEY_PPPOE only when the dissector
    requests it. The common flow_keys_dissector does not request it, so
    the typical hash path does not write it. A Documentation note may
    help; we can add one.

